    {
      "id": "t309jKEnQZaCq8znuzzEw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 854.4193413518497,
      "y": -3732.9369496644003,
      "width": 1040.5191650390625,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "adl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 155371294,
      "version": 3049,
      "versionNonce": 101179294,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792754477802,
      "created": 1792749346163,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NIC - \nNetwork Interface Card\n\nIt is like a min computer with it's own processor and memory and a very specific task.\n\nAs long as the wifi or ehternet is on, the NIC physical transceiver circuit is on.\nThe wifi NIC listens for radio signals, ethernet nic listens for wire signals\n\nWhen a voltage or radio change is detected the NIC clock locks onto the preamble (will look into this\nlater) and starts assembling the incoming bits in a FIFO buffer on it's own memory.\n\n\nIf after assembling the bits, the target MAC is not the NIC's MAC, it discards the message and never\nsends it to the CPU\n\nException being an all listen MAC address, if the message has that MAC then also it is not discarded.\n\n\nSo if the target MAC is valid and checksum passes, the message is placed in the by the NIC by process of\nDirect Memory Access (DMA). And it also tells the main CPU via an interrupt that I placed this message\nframe in your main memory, check it when you have time",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "EeUWoDKyLoPpbGNrpg8qf",
      "originalText": "NIC - \nNetwork Interface Card\n\nIt is like a min computer with it's own processor and memory and a very specific task.\n\nAs long as the wifi or ehternet is on, the NIC physical transceiver circuit is on.\nThe wifi NIC listens for radio signals, ethernet nic listens for wire signals\n\nWhen a voltage or radio change is detected the NIC clock locks onto the preamble (will look into this later) and starts assembling the incoming bits in a FIFO buffer on it's own memory.\n\n\nIf after assembling the bits, the target MAC is not the NIC's MAC, it discards the message and never sends it to the CPU\n\nException being an all listen MAC address, if the message has that MAC then also it is not discarded.\n\n\nSo if the target MAC is valid and checksum passes, the message is placed in the by the NIC by process of Direct Memory Access (DMA). And it also tells the main CPU via an interrupt that I placed this message frame in your main memory, check it when you have time",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
