      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "_gCj8hHDEaq9HVSMlfOp1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2332.8161908529764,
      "y": -1204.2080219443283,
      "width": 595.839599609375,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "l4rgD6k2Fk47Qza-Ai1_I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "adx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 23192770,
      "version": 1821,
      "versionNonce": 878188354,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792754167144,
      "created": 1792748753583,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ARP - \nAddress Resolution Protocol\n\nThe PC sends broadcast signal to every device in local\nnetwork. The mac address for this request is sent on is\nFF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF and asks if your IP is 192.168.1.1, send\nme your MAC address so I can put it in my frame to send\n\nWhen the router sends this to the PC that yeah I am\n192.168.1.1, PC saves this info in the ARP cache\n192.168.1.1 = Router MAC\n\n\nThe ARP Cache Timer: When your PC learns the router's\nMAC address via ARP, it saves that mapping in RAM with a\ncountdown timer (on Windows, dynamic ARP entries usually\nexpire after 15 to 45 seconds of inactivity, or stay alive for\na few minutes if you are actively sending traffic to that\ndevice).\n\nSo ARP cache is present in main memory of each computer\nfor each NIC card. PC has 2 for ethernet/ wifi, more if that\nhappens to be the case.\n\nRouter, which is in itself a mini computer also has 2, inside\nARP cache and outside ARP cache\n\nInsided cachec has the mapping of local devices to local IP\naddress.\n\noutside cache has mapping of the ISP router and that MAC.\nto send to the ISP",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Hg4Qh-p01WN0PXEp0g2qY",
      "originalText": "ARP - \nAddress Resolution Protocol\n\nThe PC sends broadcast signal to every device in local network. The mac address for this request is sent on is  FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF and asks if your IP is 192.168.1.1, send me your MAC address so I can put it in my frame to send\n\nWhen the router sends this to the PC that yeah I am 192.168.1.1, PC saves this info in the ARP cache\n192.168.1.1 = Router MAC\n\n\nThe ARP Cache Timer: When your PC learns the router's MAC address via ARP, it saves that mapping in RAM with a countdown timer (on Windows, dynamic ARP entries usually expire after 15 to 45 seconds of inactivity, or stay alive for a few minutes if you are actively sending traffic to that device).\n\nSo ARP cache is present in main memory of each computer for each NIC card. PC has 2 for ethernet/ wifi, more if that happens to be the case.\n\nRouter, which is in itself a mini computer also has 2, inside ARP cache and outside ARP cache\n\nInsided cachec has the mapping of local devices to local IP address.\n\noutside cache has mapping of the ISP router and that MAC.  to send to the ISP",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "_QBD4QjS66TP5gglpa6Af",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 378.2039306504919,
      "y": -1306.907152466277,
      "width": 1463.0300039378922,
      "height": 735,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "l4rgD6k2Fk47Qza-Ai1_I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ae",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 1402058078,
      "version": 664,
      "versionNonce": 758597698,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
