    {
      "id": "WrJsI57eILGvyqnbI2lau",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 391.34942578350046,
      "y": -1301.907152466277,
      "width": 1436.739013671875,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "l4rgD6k2Fk47Qza-Ai1_I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aeG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1242177666,
      "version": 945,
      "versionNonce": 1525798110,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792754167143,
      "created": 1792750010005,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FRAME (Or Ethernet Frame) - \n\nFrame is the lowest message structure that the NIC can read or send. So every NIC knows what the structure of a frame is and if the value of a\nspecific part of frame is different, what to do with that frame\n\n1. An ARP Frame:\n[ Dest MAC ] [ Source MAC ] [ Type: 0x0806 ] [      28-Byte ARP Message      ] [ FCS ]\n\n2. An Internet Data Frame (e.g., from Google):\n[ Dest MAC ] [ Source MAC ] [ Type: 0x0800 ] [ IP Packet -> TCP -> Web Data  ] [ FCS ]\n\n\nThe Outer Shell is Identical: Both have a Destination MAC, a Source MAC, and a Frame Check Sequence (FCS) at the end.\n\nThe EtherType Field (The Label): Sitting right after the Source MAC is a 2-byte code that tells your OS how to read the payload once the NIC\nplaces it in RAM:\n\n0x0806 (ARP): Tells the OS, \"Pass this payload to the ARP lookup module—there is no IP packet inside, just a 28-byte local address\nquestion/answer.\"\n\n0x0800 (IPv4) or 0x86DD (IPv6): Tells the OS, \"Pass this payload up to the IP network stack—there is an Internet Packet inside carrying\nactual data.\"\n\nWhat happens when Google sends you data:\n\nGoogle's data travels across the internet as an IP Packet.\n\nWhen that packet reaches your home Wi-Fi router (the final hop), your router wraps Google's IP Packet inside a brand-new local Frame, stamps\n0x0800 (or 0x86DD for IPv6) on the label, puts your PC's MAC as the Destination MAC, and transmits it over Wi-Fi to your NIC.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "_QBD4QjS66TP5gglpa6Af",
      "originalText": "FRAME (Or Ethernet Frame) - \n\nFrame is the lowest message structure that the NIC can read or send. So every NIC knows what the structure of a frame is and if the value of a specific part of frame is different, what to do with that frame\n\n1. An ARP Frame:\n[ Dest MAC ] [ Source MAC ] [ Type: 0x0806 ] [      28-Byte ARP Message      ] [ FCS ]\n\n2. An Internet Data Frame (e.g., from Google):\n[ Dest MAC ] [ Source MAC ] [ Type: 0x0800 ] [ IP Packet -> TCP -> Web Data  ] [ FCS ]\n\n\nThe Outer Shell is Identical: Both have a Destination MAC, a Source MAC, and a Frame Check Sequence (FCS) at the end.\n\nThe EtherType Field (The Label): Sitting right after the Source MAC is a 2-byte code that tells your OS how to read the payload once the NIC places it in RAM:\n\n0x0806 (ARP): Tells the OS, \"Pass this payload to the ARP lookup module—there is no IP packet inside, just a 28-byte local address question/answer.\"\n\n0x0800 (IPv4) or 0x86DD (IPv6): Tells the OS, \"Pass this payload up to the IP network stack—there is an Internet Packet inside carrying actual data.\"\n\nWhat happens when Google sends you data:\n\nGoogle's data travels across the internet as an IP Packet.\n\nWhen that packet reaches your home Wi-Fi router (the final hop), your router wraps Google's IP Packet inside a brand-new local Frame, stamps 0x0800 (or 0x86DD for IPv6) on the label, puts your PC's MAC as the Destination MAC, and transmits it over Wi-Fi to your NIC.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "A4FbTYFFEy_xiOfSuugKT",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": -1247.719855344148,
      "y": -1381.6137804631173,
      "width": 1319.3148870150742,
      "height": 879.7728669095127,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "l4rgD6k2Fk47Qza-Ai1_I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aeV",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 402993474,
      "version": 433,
      "versionNonce": 1701376002,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
