    {
      "id": "Dny-KuZWVCAD-MNZu7LFr",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1242.441867403017,
      "y": -1254.227347008361,
      "width": 1308.7589111328125,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "l4rgD6k2Fk47Qza-Ai1_I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "af",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1278526302,
      "version": 544,
      "versionNonce": 903842078,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792754167143,
      "created": 1792750400755,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Note on how frame is send to PC from the router - \n\nBecause Wi-Fi uses radio waves through open air, the router has no choice but to physically broadcast the signal across the room.\nEvery device on your Wi-Fi gets hit by those radio waves, reads the first 48 bits, and drops the frame in hardware—except your PC's\nNIC, which recognizes its own MAC and keeps it.\n\nThe only reason networking engineers avoid calling that a \"broadcast\" is to separate Layer 1 Physics from Layer 2 Addressing:\n\nOver Wi-Fi (Shared Air):\n\nUnicast Frame (Dest MAC = Your_PC_MAC): Physically radiates to every antenna in the room. Your phone's NIC and TV's NIC drop it\nat the chip level; only your PC's NIC copies it to RAM.\n\nBroadcast Frame (Dest MAC = FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF): Physically radiates to every antenna in the room, and every device's NIC copies\nit to RAM and wakes up its CPU.\n\nOver an Ethernet Cable (Switched Wire):\n\nIf you plug 4 PCs into a router/switch with cables, the router remembers which specific port each MAC lives on.\n\nWhen a frame is addressed to Your_PC_MAC, the switch sends electricity only down your specific wire. The other 3 PCs don't even\nget electrical signals to drop.\n\nSo on Wi-Fi, you nailed it: every frame is physically broadcast through the air, and the Destination MAC acts as the hardware filter\nthat decides whose NIC accepts it and whose NIC throws it in the bin.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "A4FbTYFFEy_xiOfSuugKT",
      "originalText": "Note on how frame is send to PC from the router - \n\nBecause Wi-Fi uses radio waves through open air, the router has no choice but to physically broadcast the signal across the room. Every device on your Wi-Fi gets hit by those radio waves, reads the first 48 bits, and drops the frame in hardware—except your PC's NIC, which recognizes its own MAC and keeps it.\n\nThe only reason networking engineers avoid calling that a \"broadcast\" is to separate Layer 1 Physics from Layer 2 Addressing:\n\nOver Wi-Fi (Shared Air):\n\nUnicast Frame (Dest MAC = Your_PC_MAC): Physically radiates to every antenna in the room. Your phone's NIC and TV's NIC drop it at the chip level; only your PC's NIC copies it to RAM.\n\nBroadcast Frame (Dest MAC = FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF): Physically radiates to every antenna in the room, and every device's NIC copies it to RAM and wakes up its CPU.\n\nOver an Ethernet Cable (Switched Wire):\n\nIf you plug 4 PCs into a router/switch with cables, the router remembers which specific port each MAC lives on.\n\nWhen a frame is addressed to Your_PC_MAC, the switch sends electricity only down your specific wire. The other 3 PCs don't even get electrical signals to drop.\n\nSo on Wi-Fi, you nailed it: every frame is physically broadcast through the air, and the Destination MAC acts as the hardware filter that decides whose NIC accepts it and whose NIC throws it in the bin.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
