    {
      "id": "sDGVlrWc03U74Fo8MAJW_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4163.687207066623,
      "y": -1677.3677861301358,
      "width": 3744.609375,
      "height": 1980,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0T",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1599088094,
      "version": 271,
      "versionNonce": 1279292994,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792754155976,
      "created": 1792752420815,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Communication between OS and NIC\n\n\nWhen packet needs sending - \n\nOS creates a payload of what type of message you want to send and wraps it in a IP packet, \n\nCommunication between the Operating System (OS) and the Network Interface Card (NIC) happens through a finely tuned choreography of shared memory, hardware registers, ring buffers, and interrupts.\n\nBecause the CPU is millions of times faster than network hardware, they cannot talk back and forth line-by-line. Instead, they use a shared workspace in your PC's RAM.\n\n1. The Shared Workspace: Ring Buffers (Descriptor Rings)\nWhen your network card driver initializes during OS bootup, it sets up two circular queues in your PC's main RAM, shared directly with the NIC:\n\nThe RX (Receive) Ring: A parking lot of empty buffer slots waiting for incoming packets from the network.\n\nThe TX (Transmit) Ring: A parking lot of filled buffer slots containing outgoing frames waiting to be sent out.\n\nEach slot in these rings is a Descriptor—a tiny metadata struct containing the memory address of the packet in RAM and its length.\n\n2. How the OS Sends Data (The TX Path)\nWhen your OS wants to send a frame:\n\nWriting to RAM: The network stack builds the complete Ethernet frame and writes it into a buffer in main RAM.\n\nUpdating the Ring: The OS writes a pointer to that RAM buffer into the next available slot in the TX Ring.\n\nRinging the Doorbell: The OS writes a value to a physical control register on the NIC chip (Memory-Mapped I/O). This is literally telling the NIC: \"Hey, I added 2 new packets to the TX ring, go get them.\"\n\nDMA (Direct Memory Access): The NIC's onboard controller takes over. It uses DMA to read the pointers from the TX ring, fetches the frame directly out of system RAM over the motherboard bus, converts the\nbits, and beams them out. The CPU is already free doing other tasks.\n\n3. How the OS Receives Data (The RX Path)\nWhen data arrives from the network:\n\nPre-allocated Buffers: The OS keeps the RX ring constantly populated with empty RAM buffers so the NIC always has somewhere to put incoming traffic.\n\nDirect Drop via DMA: The NIC catches incoming bits, assembles the frame, and uses DMA to copy it directly into the pre-allocated RAM buffer specified by the next open slot in the RX Ring.\n\nThe Interrupt (Waking the OS): Once the frame lands safely in RAM, the NIC fires a Hardware Interrupt down the motherboard bus to alert the CPU.\n\nDriver Processing: The OS driver stops what it is doing, reads the frame out of the RX ring, updates the ring pointers, and passes the packet up to the OS network stack (TCP/IP).\n\n(Modern high-speed NICs often use Polling / NAPI instead of firing an interrupt for every single packet to prevent CPU overload during heavy traffic spikes).",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "44LVjdOi1v4TCZv7SJMsN",
      "originalText": "Communication between OS and NIC\n\n\nWhen packet needs sending - \n\nOS creates a payload of what type of message you want to send and wraps it in a IP packet, \n\nCommunication between the Operating System (OS) and the Network Interface Card (NIC) happens through a finely tuned choreography of shared memory, hardware registers, ring buffers, and interrupts.\n\nBecause the CPU is millions of times faster than network hardware, they cannot talk back and forth line-by-line. Instead, they use a shared workspace in your PC's RAM.\n\n1. The Shared Workspace: Ring Buffers (Descriptor Rings)\nWhen your network card driver initializes during OS bootup, it sets up two circular queues in your PC's main RAM, shared directly with the NIC:\n\nThe RX (Receive) Ring: A parking lot of empty buffer slots waiting for incoming packets from the network.\n\nThe TX (Transmit) Ring: A parking lot of filled buffer slots containing outgoing frames waiting to be sent out.\n\nEach slot in these rings is a Descriptor—a tiny metadata struct containing the memory address of the packet in RAM and its length.\n\n2. How the OS Sends Data (The TX Path)\nWhen your OS wants to send a frame:\n\nWriting to RAM: The network stack builds the complete Ethernet frame and writes it into a buffer in main RAM.\n\nUpdating the Ring: The OS writes a pointer to that RAM buffer into the next available slot in the TX Ring.\n\nRinging the Doorbell: The OS writes a value to a physical control register on the NIC chip (Memory-Mapped I/O). This is literally telling the NIC: \"Hey, I added 2 new packets to the TX ring, go get them.\"\n\nDMA (Direct Memory Access): The NIC's onboard controller takes over. It uses DMA to read the pointers from the TX ring, fetches the frame directly out of system RAM over the motherboard bus, converts the bits, and beams them out. The CPU is already free doing other tasks.\n\n3. How the OS Receives Data (The RX Path)\nWhen data arrives from the network:\n\nPre-allocated Buffers: The OS keeps the RX ring constantly populated with empty RAM buffers so the NIC always has somewhere to put incoming traffic.\n\nDirect Drop via DMA: The NIC catches incoming bits, assembles the frame, and uses DMA to copy it directly into the pre-allocated RAM buffer specified by the next open slot in the RX Ring.\n\nThe Interrupt (Waking the OS): Once the frame lands safely in RAM, the NIC fires a Hardware Interrupt down the motherboard bus to alert the CPU.\n\nDriver Processing: The OS driver stops what it is doing, reads the frame out of the RX ring, updates the ring pointers, and passes the packet up to the OS network stack (TCP/IP).\n\n(Modern high-speed NICs often use Polling / NAPI instead of firing an interrupt for every single packet to prevent CPU overload during heavy traffic spikes).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
