    {
      "id": "0JOCXpZIXSdf19daZGKt8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3705.7526998044814,
      "y": -4981.256566996491,
      "width": 1083.68359375,
      "height": 1330,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0wV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2062669790,
      "version": 290,
      "versionNonce": 745658014,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792755203108,
      "created": 1792755124793,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Client device - PC, phone etc\n\ncore networking tasks include:\n\n1. Application Interface (Sockets API):\nProvides the programming interface (like TCP/UDP sockets) so apps like your\nweb browser or ping can hand raw data streams to the OS and ask them to be\nsent over the network.\n\n2. Transport Layer Management (TCP/UDP):\nManages connections by executing the 3-way handshake, tracking sequence\nnumbers, enforcing flow control, handling port assignments, and retransmitting\nlost packets if data gets dropped.\n\n3. Network Layer & IP Construction:\nAssembles the IP packet, sets the source and destination IP addresses,\nmanages TTL (Time to Live) decrementing, and checks the OS routing table to\ndecide if a packet goes to the local network or the default gateway.\n\n4. Address Resolution (ARP / NDP):\nRuns background ARP (IPv4) or NDP (IPv6) protocols to query the local\nnetwork, map local IP addresses to physical MAC addresses, and maintain the\nlocal ARP cache in system RAM.\n\n5. Data Link Framing:\nExplicitly builds the complete Ethernet frame by writing the Destination MAC,\nSource MAC, EtherType label, payload, and FCS checksum directly into system\nRAM.\n\n6. Driver & Ring Buffer Coordination:\nInteracts with the NIC driver to place outgoing frames into the TX ring\nbuffer, ring the hardware doorbell, and process incoming interrupts or RX ring\nbuffers from the NIC via DMA.\n\n7. Host Firewall & Packet Filtering:\nInspects incoming and outgoing packets at the software level (e.g., Windows\nDefender Firewall or Linux iptables) to block unauthorized connection attempts\nbefore applications ever see them.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "j2AT5aZIMujV6cCfj16Fw",
      "originalText": "Client device - PC, phone etc\n\ncore networking tasks include:\n\n1. Application Interface (Sockets API):\nProvides the programming interface (like TCP/UDP sockets) so apps like your web browser or ping can hand raw data streams to the OS and ask them to be sent over the network.\n\n2. Transport Layer Management (TCP/UDP):\nManages connections by executing the 3-way handshake, tracking sequence numbers, enforcing flow control, handling port assignments, and retransmitting lost packets if data gets dropped.\n\n3. Network Layer & IP Construction:\nAssembles the IP packet, sets the source and destination IP addresses, manages TTL (Time to Live) decrementing, and checks the OS routing table to decide if a packet goes to the local network or the default gateway.\n\n4. Address Resolution (ARP / NDP):\nRuns background ARP (IPv4) or NDP (IPv6) protocols to query the local network, map local IP addresses to physical MAC addresses, and maintain the local ARP cache in system RAM.\n\n5. Data Link Framing:\nExplicitly builds the complete Ethernet frame by writing the Destination MAC, Source MAC, EtherType label, payload, and FCS checksum directly into system RAM.\n\n6. Driver & Ring Buffer Coordination:\nInteracts with the NIC driver to place outgoing frames into the TX ring buffer, ring the hardware doorbell, and process incoming interrupts or RX ring buffers from the NIC via DMA.\n\n7. Host Firewall & Packet Filtering:\nInspects incoming and outgoing packets at the software level (e.g., Windows Defender Firewall or Linux iptables) to block unauthorized connection attempts before applications ever see them.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
