    {
      "id": "FIwt4GnAGM5cKKfsS5uHm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 14608.032089824294,
      "y": -2003.5349335446936,
      "width": 2625.587158203125,
      "height": 1170,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1380216414,
      "version": 115,
      "versionNonce": 1917917214,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792757704121,
      "created": 1792757687827,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Books to read\n\n\nTo build a rock-solid mental model of everything from physical NIC frames to global BGP routing, three books stand out as the absolute gold\nstandards:\n\n1. \"Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach\" by James F. Kurose and Keith W. Ross\n\nWhy it fits: This is widely considered the best conceptual book on networking. Instead of starting with dry hardware specs, it takes a \"top-down\"\napproach—starting from applications and working its way down through transport, network (IP/routers), and data link (MAC/ARP) layers.\n\n2. \"Network Warrior\" by Gary Donahue\n\nWhy it fits: If you want to bridge the gap between home routers and real-world enterprise/ISP edge hardware (Cisco, routing tables, ASICs, VLANs),\nthis book cuts through the heavy academic theory and explains how networking actually works in production.\n\n3. \"TCP/IP Illustrated, Volume 1\" by W. Richard Stevens\n\nWhy it fits: The ultimate bible for low-level protocol mechanics. If you want to trace every single byte of an IP packet, see how ARP caches behave\nunder the hood, and look at actual packet captures, this is the definitive reference.\n\nMost of these are easily available as physical books or digital PDFs through standard technical libraries.\n\nWant a breakdown of specific online labs or free resources to practice capturing and viewing live packets yourself?\n\nYes",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "jGjnwwn4INK5OEBZkdX12",
      "originalText": "Books to read\n\n\nTo build a rock-solid mental model of everything from physical NIC frames to global BGP routing, three books stand out as the absolute gold standards:\n\n1. \"Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach\" by James F. Kurose and Keith W. Ross\n\nWhy it fits: This is widely considered the best conceptual book on networking. Instead of starting with dry hardware specs, it takes a \"top-down\" approach—starting from applications and working its way down through transport, network (IP/routers), and data link (MAC/ARP) layers.\n\n2. \"Network Warrior\" by Gary Donahue\n\nWhy it fits: If you want to bridge the gap between home routers and real-world enterprise/ISP edge hardware (Cisco, routing tables, ASICs, VLANs), this book cuts through the heavy academic theory and explains how networking actually works in production.\n\n3. \"TCP/IP Illustrated, Volume 1\" by W. Richard Stevens\n\nWhy it fits: The ultimate bible for low-level protocol mechanics. If you want to trace every single byte of an IP packet, see how ARP caches behave under the hood, and look at actual packet captures, this is the definitive reference.\n\nMost of these are easily available as physical books or digital PDFs through standard technical libraries.\n\nWant a breakdown of specific online labs or free resources to practice capturing and viewing live packets yourself?\n\nYes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    }
